module lib/basis/basis
  The table every basis-set module of lib/basis returns, the `Basis` record,
  and the helpers that read it. A basis set gives each element a list of
  shells; a shell is one angular momentum `l` and a contraction, a fixed sum
  of Gaussians $\sum_p c_p e^{-a_p r^2}$ with exponents $a_p$ and coefficients
  $c_p$. The record keeps shells and their Gaussians (primitives) as two
  arrays, so a generated module can hold a whole set as two flat lists of
  numbers.

basis_rows  @1e19e0d9
  why   The generated modules store each table as one flat list, which is the
        cheapest thing to write and to parse; the rest of the code indexes
        rows and columns.
  v      the flat list, row after row
  width  how many columns a row has
  n      how many rows the list holds
  a      the table, `[n, width]`
  i      the row being filled
  j      the column being filled

basis_of  @69c81243
  why   Every generated module ends in this one call, so the layout of the
        record is decided in one place.
  shells_flat  the shell rows `(Z, l, nprim, p0)`, flattened
  prims_flat   the primitive rows `(exponent, coefficient)`, flattened

basis_first  @769786bc
  why   A molecule copies an element's shells by starting at its first row
        and reading while the element stays the same, which works because
        each element's shells are one run of rows (basis_check holds the
        table to that).
  b   the basis set
  z   the atomic number looked for
  s   the shell row being tested

basis_count  @fbbb0e29
  why   Used by this module's test to check a table's layout.
  b   the basis set
  z   the atomic number

basis_nao  @563056d6
  why   A shell of angular momentum `l` gives $(l+1)(l+2)/2$ Cartesian
        functions and $2l+1$ spherical ones; the counts per atom let the
        generated tests compare the table with the counts the generator
        wrote down.
  b     the basis set
  z     the atomic number
  out   `[Cartesian count, spherical count]`, summed over the element's shells
  mine  1 for each shell row of element `z`, 0 for the others
  l     each shell row's angular momentum

basis_check  @1d6ea925
  why   A table that breaks its layout would give a molecule wrong shells
        without any error: a shell reading another shell's primitives, or an
        element's shells split so basis_first finds only part of them. Every
        generated table's test requires this count to be `0`.
  b     the basis set
  bad   the number of problems found so far
  next  the primitive row the next shell must start at, if no row is skipped
        or shared
  s     the shell row being checked
